        "title": "The Mongol Empire - Unification, Conquest, Divergence",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "```\nCONQUEST TIMELINE:\n  1209–1215: Xi Xia and Jin Dynasty (northern China) — war begins\n  1219–1221: Khwarazmian Empire (modern Uzbekistan/Iran/Afghanistan)\n    Shah Muhammad II refused to negotiate after Mongol merchants\n    were killed. Response: total destruction.\n    Samarkand, Bukhara, Merv, Nishapur — great cities annihilated\n    Merv: reported ~700,000 killed (probably exaggerated; still catastrophic)\n  1223: Battle of the Kalka River — first contact with Rus\n  1227: Genghis dies (campaign against Xi Xia; reason unclear)\n  1230–1234: Final conquest of Jin (northern China)\n  1236–1241: Batu Khan's western campaign\n    1237: Ryazan, Vladimir (Russia)\n    1240: Kyiv sacked\n    1241: Battle of Legnica (Poland), Battle of Mohi (Hungary)\n    Withdrew: Ögedei died; succession required return\n  1253–1258: Hulagu's campaign west\n    1258: Baghdad sacked; Abbasid caliphate ended\n    1260: Ain Jalut — stopped by Mamluks"
    },
